<h2>Two statutes, one route</h2>
<p>Section 28 of the Hindu Marriage Act makes all decrees under the Act appealable as decrees of the court in civil suits, and makes orders under Sections 25 (permanent alimony) and 26 (custody and maintenance of children) appealable where they are not interim. The Family Courts Act then supplies the forum: Section 19 provides an appeal from every judgment or order (not being interlocutory) of a Family Court, to the High Court, heard by a Bench of two judges. In Delhi, where Family Courts function in every district, this means the practical appellate route from a divorce decree is a MAT.APP.(F.C.) before a Division Bench of the Delhi High Court — not a regular first appeal before a single judge.</p>

<h2>What is appealable — and what is not</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Appealable</strong><p>Decrees of divorce, judicial separation, restitution and nullity; dismissals of such petitions; orders of permanent alimony under Section 25; final custody orders under Section 26.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Not appealable</strong><p>Interlocutory orders — adjournments, procedural directions, interim arrangements of a transient character. Section 19(1) excludes them expressly; Section 28(2) excludes interim orders under Sections 25 and 26.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Grey zone</strong><p>Interim maintenance under Section 24 HMA: substantial orders deciding entitlement have been treated as appealable judgments, while routine implementation directions have not. The test is effect, not label.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>No appeal on costs</strong><p>Section 28(3) bars appeals on the subject of costs only.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The 90-day clock and the remarriage trap</h2>
<p>The limitation period for a matrimonial appeal is ninety days from the decree — tripled from the original thirty by amendment precisely because the consequences of a missed appeal are irreversible. Section 15 HMA ties remarriage to this clock: after a divorce decree, either party may lawfully remarry only when the time for appealing has expired without an appeal being presented, or after an appeal has been dismissed. A spouse who remarries within the window gambles on the appeal never being filed; if it is filed and succeeds, the second marriage is cast into doubt and the litigation multiplies. Advising a client who holds a fresh decree therefore involves two dates: the date the certified copy is applied for, and the ninety-first day.</p>

<h2>How the Division Bench approaches matrimonial appeals</h2>
<p>A matrimonial appeal is a first appeal, and the Division Bench is entitled to reappreciate the entire evidence — cruelty findings, desertion timelines, condonation, the quality of proof on adultery or mental disorder. In practice, three patterns dominate. First, appellate courts are slow to disturb findings of fact that rest on the trial judge\'s assessment of oral testimony, so appeals built purely on "the Family Court believed the wrong witness" rarely succeed. Second, appeals succeed most often on legal error: a ground not pleaded but decreed, cruelty found on incidents that were condoned, or desertion computed without the statutory two-year period. Third, the Bench frequently recalibrates the financial package even while affirming the decree — permanent alimony under Section 25 is the most commonly modified component. Interim stays are possible, but courts weigh the hardship of freezing matrimonial status against the appellant\'s prospects.</p>

<h2>Practical sequencing for the losing party</h2>
<p>Apply for the certified copy the day the judgment is pronounced; the copying period is excluded from limitation. Decide early whether the challenge is to the decree itself or only its financial terms, because a focused appeal on alimony and custody reads very differently from a root-and-branch attack. If settlement is realistic, the appellate stage is often the best moment — the parties finally know their litigation worth — and consent terms can be recorded by the Division Bench. And if the decision is not to appeal, communicate that clearly: the other side\'s remarriage plans, and your client\'s, hang on it.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This is a general explainer on appeals in matrimonial matters under the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 and the Family Courts Act, 1984, and is not legal advice. Analogous appeal provisions exist under other personal laws and the Special Marriage Act, with their own particulars.</p>
</div>
